    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d698">

                <pb facs="0682" n="674" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg674"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.48/108: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-02-11T09:00:00+02:00">February 11, 1941—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 12—10:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. I have received an official request orally and in writing from the French
                    High Commissioner<anchor n="22a" xml:id="d698-f1"/> to use my good offices with
                    the British authorities with a view to obtaining permission for the importation
                    into Syria in 1941 of some 1200 cases of Nestle’s sweetened and unsweetened milk
                    from the United States. He states this is urgently needed to prevent serious
                    undernourishment of children both native and foreign. He adds that distribution
                    will be closely supervised under medical certificates and he offers to give
                    guarantees regarding such distribution.</p>
                <p>I have informally promised to submit the matter to the Department to discuss it
                    with the British Consul General.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d698-f1" n="22a" xml:id="d698-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Henri-Fernand Dentz.</p>
            </note>
